The SPA fallback problem: the usual `/* /index.html 200` rewrite (and Cloudflare Pages' automatic SPA mode when there is no root 404.html) answers EVERY unknown URL with 200, which RTE-004 forbids. So: (1) prerender every public route to its own `<route>/index.html` with @prerenderer/rollup-plugin (framework-agnostic; installs: `npm i -D @prerenderer/rollup-plugin @prerenderer/renderer-puppeteer puppeteer`) or, for Vue, `vite-ssg`; (2) add NO catch-all rewrite; (3) turn the prerendered `/404` route into the root `404.html`, which the host serves for URLs with no file; (4) the only allowed fallback is a scoped rewrite for auth-gated client routes (`/app/*`). Verify after deploying: `curl -sI https://example.com/sg-probe` must say 404 and `/about/` 200. The 404 page is your React layout (nav + footer) with `<meta name="robots" content="noindex">` (React 19 hoists it into head) and a link to `/`. 410: Cloudflare `_redirects` supports only 301/302/303/307/308 and 200, and Netlify lists 301/302/404/200, so retired paths (only if you retire URLs) use a tiny edge function listing them: Netlify `netlify/edge-functions/gone.js` or Cloudflare `functions/_middleware.js` plus a publish-dir `_routes.json` with `{"version":1,"include":["/old-product","/old-section/*"],"exclude":[]}`; both serve the prerendered `/410` page (route below) with status 410. Self-hosted instead: nginx `location = /old-product { return 410; }`, Apache `Redirect gone "/old-product"`. A static host cannot return a 5xx for your pages: the 5xx belongs to the API service; the SPA must turn a failed data fetch into an error state, never an empty 200-looking success page.
